  "text": "Question seven. What is a shuffle, and why is it expensive? It moves rows so that every row with one key lands in one partition. Map tasks write their output to local disk, and reduce tasks fetch it over the network. From the page's latency chart: an SSD read takes a thousand times as long as memory, and a network round trip a hundred thousand times. If the fastest cache took one second, a round trip would take a hundred sixteen days. So tuning Spark is mostly about fewer shuffles, with less data in each.\n\nQuestion eight. What is a stage and a task in Spark's execution model? One action starts a job. Spark cuts it into stages at every wide dependency, so counting shuffles counts stages. Each stage is a set of tasks, one per partition, running the same narrow steps in parallel, and it ends only when its last task does. From the page's fan-out chart: if each task has a one percent chance of being slow, a stage of sixty-nine tasks is slow half the time, and one of two hundred, eighty-seven percent of the time.",
